    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion, my friend. Let's get back to the nuts and bolts of your walnut cabinet joinery. When dealing with the grain, it's crucial to maintain a consistent angle and pressure as you cut your dovetails. The grain can be unpredictable, like a mischievous goblin, but with the right technique, you can tame it.\n\nHere are some practical steps to help you manage the grain and prevent chipping:\n\n1. Use a sharp chisel: A sharp chisel will make cleaner cuts and reduce the chances of splintering. Sharpen your chisel regularly with a whetstone to maintain a fine edge.\n\n2. Maintain a consistent angle: Keep your chisel at a consistent angle as you cut the dovetails. This will help you avoid catching the grain at an awkward angle, which can cause splintering.\n\n3. Apply even pressure: Apply even pressure as you cut the dovetails. Too much pressure can cause the wood to splinter, while too little pressure can result in an uneven cut.\n\n4. Use a dovetail jig: A dovetail jig can help you maintain consistent angles and pressure as you cut the dovetails. This can help you avoid splintering and make your work more precise",
